<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · War and march · 1921 · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Independence March</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="tr">İstiklal Marşı</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The national anthem of Turkey, written in 1921 for the soldiers of the Turkish War of Independence.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Mehmet Âkif Ersoy</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Osman Zeki Üngör (1930; orchestrated by Edgar Manas, 1932)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Turkish National Movement; the Republic of Turkey and the Turkish Republic of Northern Cyprus</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Turkey (Grand National Assembly)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words adopted 12 March 1921; music adopted 1930</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Mehmet Âkif Ersoy (adopted 1921; he died in 1936))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;İstiklal Marşı&quot; (&quot;Independence March&quot;) is the national anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a> and of the Turkish Republic of Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. It was made during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">Turkish War of Independence</a>, when the Turkish National Movement led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a> was fighting both invading foreign powers and the Ottoman court, which it held to be complicit in the partition of the Turkish homeland after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sevres">Treaty of Sèvres</a> of 1920.</p><p>In 1921 the movement organised a nationwide competition for a National March, meant to encourage the forces fighting for independence and to give pride to the homeland that would be founded after victory. 724 poems were sent in. Mehmet Âkif Ersoy, a well-known poet of the time, at first refused to take part because the competition offered a money prize, but the National Parliament persuaded him to submit a poem and set the reward aside; the prize later went to a society of veterans. His ten-stanza poem was read to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> by the deputy Hamdullah Suphi on 1 March 1921 and, after a parliamentary committee had examined it, unanimously adopted. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-national-assembly-of-turkey">Grand National Assembly</a> officially adopted it as the anthem on 12 March 1921, two and a half years before the Republic was proclaimed on 29 October 1923. Ersoy's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> is dedicated &quot;To Our Heroic Army&quot;, the national forces that went on to win the war.</p><p>The music took longer to settle. The first setting, by a composer named Çağatay, lasted only six years. In 1930 Parliament adopted a new composition by Osman Zeki Üngör, a virtuoso and the first conductor of the Presidential Symphony Orchestra, and in 1932 the government commissioned the musician Edgar Manas to harmonise and orchestrate Üngör's melody, which gave the anthem its final official form.</p><p>The anthem has 41 lines in ten stanzas, but only the first two are sung, at state and military events, national festivals and bayrams, sporting events and school ceremonies. In Turkish classrooms a framed copy usually hangs above the blackboard, beside the Turkish flag, a photograph of Atatürk and his address to the nation's youth from his speech to Parliament of 20 October 1927. The first two stanzas appeared on the back of the Turkish 100 lira banknotes of 1983–1989. In 1983 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Turkish Republic</a> of Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> adopted it as its own anthem under Article II of its constitution.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A poem of love for the Turkish homeland, its flag and its soil, and of faith and freedom. It praises hope, devotion and sacrifice in the struggle for independence and honours the soldiers who fought for it; the mood is solemn and defiant.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Turkish</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Turkish</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Korkma, sönmez bu şafaklarda yüzen al sancak;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Do not fear; the crimson banner that waves in these dawns will not fade</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Sönmeden yurdumun üstünde tüten en son ocak.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>before the last hearth that smokes over my homeland is put out.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">O benim milletimin yıldızıdır, parlayacak;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is the star of my nation; it will shine;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">O benimdir, o benim milletimindir ancak.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>it is mine, and it belongs to my nation alone.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Çatma, kurban olayım, çehreni ey nazlı hilâl</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Do not frown, may I be your sacrifice, O coy crescent,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Kahraman ırkıma bir gül! Ne bu şiddet, bu celâl?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>smile on my heroic race! Why this violence, this wrath?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Sana olmaz dökülen kanlarımız sonra helâl..</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Otherwise the blood we have shed will not be lawful to you then.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Hakkıdır, Hakk'a tapan milletimin istiklâl.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Independence is the right of my nation, which worships God.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Ben ezelden beridir hür yaşadım, hür yaşarım</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I have lived free since time began, and free I live.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Hangi çılgın bana zincir uracakmış? Şaşarım!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What madman would put chains on me? I am astonished!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Kükremiş sel gibiyim; bendimi çiğner aşarım;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I am like a roaring flood; I trample my dam and surge over it;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Yırtarım dağları, enginlere sığmam, taşarım.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I tear the mountains, I do not fit into the open expanses, I overflow.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Garbın âfâkını sarmışsa çelik zırhlı duvar,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If the horizons of the West are walled in by armour of steel,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Benim iman dolu göğsüm gibi serhaddim var.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I have a frontier like my breast full of faith.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Ulusun, korkma! Nasıl böyle bir imanı boğar,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let it howl, do not fear! How could it strangle such a faith,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">&quot;Medeniyet&quot; dediğin tek dişi kalmış canavar?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that one-toothed monster you call 'civilisation'?</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 41 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Arkadaş yurduma alçakları uğratma, sakın</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Friend, do not let the vile ones reach my homeland, beware;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Siper et gövdeni, dursun bu hayasızca akın.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>make your body a shield, let this shameless onslaught stop.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Doğacaktır sana vadettiği günler Hakk'ın..</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The days that God promised you will dawn;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Kim bilir belki, yarın; belki yarından da yakın.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who knows, perhaps tomorrow; perhaps even sooner than tomorrow.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Bastığın yerleri &quot;toprak?&quot; diyerek geçme, tanı!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Do not pass over the ground you tread on, calling it 'soil': know it!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Düşün altındaki binlerce kefensiz yatanı,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Think of the thousands lying beneath it without a shroud.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Sen şehid oğlusun. İncitme, yazıktır, atanı;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You are the son of a martyr. Do not hurt your forefather, it would be a shame;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Verme dünyaları alsan da bu cennet vatanı.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>do not give this paradise of a homeland, even if you were to gain worlds.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Kim bu cennet vatanın uğruna olmaz ki feda?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who would not be sacrificed for the sake of this paradise of a homeland?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Şühedâ fışkıracak toprağı sıksan şühedâ!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Martyrs would gush out if you squeezed its soil, martyrs!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Canı, cananı, bütün varımı alsın da Hudâ,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let God take my life, my beloved, all that I have,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Etmesin tek vatanımdan beni dünyada cüdâ.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>but let Him not part me in this world from my only homeland.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Ruhumun senden, İlâhi, şudur ancak emeli:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O my God, the one wish of my soul from You is this:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Değmesin mâbedimin göğsüne nâmahrem eli.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let no stranger's hand touch the breast of my temple.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Bu ezanlar -ki şahâdetleri dinin temeli-</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>These calls to prayer, whose testimonies are the foundation of the faith,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Ebedî yurdumun üstünde benim inlemeli.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>must ring out over my eternal homeland.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">O zaman vecd ile bin secde eder -varsa- taşım,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then my gravestone, if I have one, will bow down a thousand times in ecstasy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Her cerihamdan İlâhi, boşanıp kanlı yaşım,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O my God, my bloody tears pouring from every wound of mine;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Fışkırır ruh-ı mücerred gibi yerden naşım;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my corpse will spring from the earth like a disembodied spirit;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">O zaman yükselerek arşa değer belki başım.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>then, rising, my head will perhaps touch the Throne.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Dalgalan sen de şafaklar gibi ey şanlı hilâl!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Wave you too like the dawns, O glorious crescent!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Olsun artık dökülen kanlarımın hepsi helâl.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let all the blood I have shed now be lawful at last.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Ebediyyen sana yok, ırkıma yok izmihlâl:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Forever there is no perishing for you, nor for my race:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Hakkıdır hür yaşamış bayrağımın hürriyet;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>freedom is the right of my flag, which has lived free;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Hakkıdır Hakk'a tapan milletimin istiklâl.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>independence is the right of my nation, which worships God.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Mehmet Âkif Ersoy (1921). Text from <a href="https://tr.wikisource.org/wiki/%C4%B0stikl%C3%A2l_Mar%C5%9F%C4%B1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (12)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'sönmez' is 'will not go out, be extinguished', the word used of fire and light, as in line 1.2.</li><li>line 2.1: 'kurban olayım', 'may I be your sacrifice', an endearment; 'nazlı hilâl', the coy (or proud, petted) crescent of the flag.</li><li>line 2.3: 'helâl' is what is religiously lawful, and also what is given or forgiven ungrudgingly: the blood shed for the flag would not be blessed or freely given to it.</li><li>lines 2.4 and 10.5: 'Hakk' is God (the Truth, the Right), a play on 'hakkı', 'its right'.</li><li>line 3.4: 'enginler' are open plains or the open sea; 'taşarım', 'I overflow, spill over'.</li><li>line 4.4: 'tek dişi kalmış canavar', 'the monster with only one tooth left', i.e. a decrepit beast.</li><li>line 6.3: 'atanı', 'your forefather (ancestor)'.</li><li>line 8.2: 'nâmahrem', one who is not of the family and may not enter its private quarters; 'mâbed', a temple or place of worship.</li><li>line 8.4: 'inlemeli', literally 'must moan, resound'.</li><li>line 9.4: 'arş', the Throne of God in the highest heaven.</li><li>line 10.3: 'izmihlâl', perishing, ruin, annihilation.</li><li>line 4.3: 'Ulusun' is read as 'let it howl' (ulumak, to howl), said of the monster of the next line.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
